1. warn writes to stderr, not stdout
   warn "careful"; puts "out"
      stdout: out
      stderr: careful
      exit status: 0
2. uplevel: adds the file, the line and the word warning
   warn "old api", uplevel: 0
      stderr: -e:1: warning: old api
      exit status: 0
3. Warning[:deprecated] is off by default; -W:deprecated turns it on
   ruby -e '...'
      stdout: Warning[:deprecated] = false
      exit status: 0
   ruby -W:deprecated -e '...'
      stdout: Warning[:deprecated] = true
      stderr: old
      exit status: 0
4. $VERBOSE is false by default, true under -w, nil under -W0 (which silences warn)
   ruby -e 'p $VERBOSE; warn "shown?"'
      stdout: false
      stderr: shown?
      exit status: 0
   ruby -w -e '...'
      stdout: true
      stderr: shown?
      exit status: 0
   ruby -W0 -e '...'
      stdout: nil
      exit status: 0
5. the warning categories and their defaults
   Warning.categories [:deprecated, :experimental, :performance, :strict_unused_block]
   Warning[:deprecated]             false
   Warning[:experimental]           true
   Warning[:performance]            false
   Warning[:strict_unused_block]    false
6. what -w reports on a sloppy file, and -c, the syntax check
   ruby sloppy.rb
      stdout: literal condition
      stdout: hi again
      exit status: 0
   ruby -w sloppy.rb
      stdout: literal condition
      stdout: hi again
      stderr: sloppy.rb:2: warning: assigned but unused variable - unused
      stderr: sloppy.rb:10: warning: literal in condition
      stderr: sloppy.rb:16: warning: mismatched indentations at 'end' with 'def' at 14
      stderr: sloppy.rb:15: warning: assigned but unused variable - x
      stderr: sloppy.rb:6: warning: method redefined; discarding old greet
      stderr: sloppy.rb:1: warning: previous definition of greet was here
      exit status: 0
   ruby -c sloppy.rb
      stdout: Syntax OK
      exit status: 0
7. capturing warnings in-process by prepending to Warning
   captured [["captured one", nil], ["captured three", :deprecated]]
   (the second never reached Warning.warn: its category was off at the time)
8. mutating a string literal warns only under -W:deprecated (literals are chilled)
   ruby -e 's = "lit"; s << "x"; puts s'
      stdout: litx
      exit status: 0
   ruby -W:deprecated -e '...'
      stdout: litx
      stderr: -e:1: warning: literal string will be frozen in the future (run with --debug-frozen-string-literal for more information)
      exit status: 0
